{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DXWQakvvbJZXfFrxEoQUA5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPAYLOAD 8/32: review of v1.4.3-rc.1..0e687e3c5285\n2\t\n3\tScope read. The new PLAN-197 split modules are `.claude/hooks/_lib/_context_budget_parts/{middle_out,status,summarization}.py`, `_cost_transcripts_parts/{__init__,cli,pricing,records,rollup,routing,routing_helpers}.py` and `_installer_write_safety/{__init__,commands,engine,model,operands}.py`. I also read the three entry points that load them (`.claude/scripts/context-budget.py:199-203`, `.claude/scripts/ceo-cost-transcripts.py:248-252`, `.claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py:23-32`), the sibling modules they bind together (inventory/compaction, syntax/verdicts_a/verdicts_b/report), the fail-soft loader `ceo-cost.py:483-510`, and the CI gate `validate.yml:411-435`.\n4\t\n5\tLimit of this review: I had read-only file tools and no shell. Every finding below comes from reading the code against CPython 3.9 semantics. Each one comes with a short reproduction the maintainers can run.\n6\t\n7\tDeclared conditions this payload touches:\n8\t- Condition 7: true for these three entry points. They keep their paths, and the logic now lives in `_lib` packages.\n9\t- Condition 5: none of these three packages is in the 13 new kernel lines, and the condition does not claim them.\n10\t- Condition 13: every path in this payload is in scope.\n11\t\n12\tNo declared condition is contradicted, and I found no P0.\n13\t\n14\tAdopter blast radius:\n15\t- No hook, settings template, model, effort or session gate changes in this payload.\n16\t- `context-budget.py` is an advisory CLI behind `/context-budget`.\n17\t- `ceo-cost-transcripts.py` is loaded fail-soft by `ceo-cost.py:495-510`.\n18\t- `check-installer-write-safety.py` is a maintainer CI census (`validate.yml:411-414`, `set -euo pipefail`), so any crash leaves CI red.\n19\t- `install_lib_selective` (`scripts/install.sh:1520-1547`) copies each top-level `_lib` entry, so the three new packages ship on install.\n20\t\n21\tPython 3.9 and stdlib-only: confirmed. No 3.10+ syntax or APIs. Imports are standard library plus `_lib.runtime_paths`, `_lib.agent_frontmatter` and `_lib.injection_patterns`.\n22\t\n23\tName resolution: every name the split modules leave undefined resolves in the entry point's namespace after binding:\n24\t- context-budget: `_env_int` and `_clamp_pct` come from compaction, `_clamp_nonneg_int` from summarization, `estimate_tokens` and `_read_text` from inventory. `CAT_MCP` and `THRESHOLD_SKILL_LINES` are defined in the entry point.\n25\t- cost-transcripts: the entry point defines `_SCRIPT_DIR` and `_rp`, and imports every stdlib name the binder excludes.\n26\t\n27\tNo duplicate top-level definitions across components, so binding order does not change which definition wins.\n28\t\n29\tNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n30\t\n31\tP1. The installer-census entry point splits the ParseError/Incomplete exception hierarchy.\n32\t- Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:36`.\n33\t- Cause: `_bind_class` rebuilds every package class with `type(original)(original.__name__, original.__bases__, attrs)`, keeping the original bases. In the entry point's namespace, `ParseError` becomes a fresh clone. `Incomplete` (`syntax.py:10`, `class Incomplete(ParseError)`) becomes a clone whose base is the template `syntax.ParseError`, not that clone. So in the entry point, `issubclass(Incomplete, ParseError)` is now False; in the old single-file script it was True.\n34\t- Effect: the rebound `_read_dquote` and `_read_balanced` raise the `Incomplete` clone (`syntax.py:44,50,63,75,89,93`), and every rebound `except ParseError:` stops catching it: `operands.py:35`, `commands.py:131`, `model.py:320,344,632`, `verdicts_b.py:141,440,447`. Only `syntax.py:270`, which names `Incomplete` itself, still catches it.\n35\t- Failure scenario: a scanned `scripts/**/*.sh` line such as `sort --output='a\"b' in.txt`, `curl --output='x\"y' \"$u\"` or `cp --target-directory='d\"x' f`.\n36\t  1. The tokenizer accepts the line.\n37\t  2. `_classify_output_mode` (`commands.py:571`) or the writer branch (`commands.py:478`) rebuilds the canonical value `a\"b` as a new token.\n38\t  3. `_record_command` → `_record_occ` → `canon_operand` → `_read_dquote` raises `Incomplete`, which its `except ParseError` no longer catches.\n39\t  4. The exception escapes `build_file_model` and `run_census` (no broad except anywhere in the package). The census dies with a traceback and exit 1, emitting no site and naming no file.\n40\t  \n41\t  The old script fell back and kept scanning. The same applies to `_expansion_refs` on a sed side holding `${x:-it's}`.\n42\t- Fail direction: still closed, because the CI gate goes red. But the instrument's own contract is broken: an input it cannot handle should become a blocking site, and it now becomes a crash.\n43\t- It also contradicts CHANGELOG.md:112 (\"Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts\"), which names `check-installer-write-safety.py` at :116-117. The entry point's docstring also promises downstream harnesses the parser helpers, and `try: m.lex(t) except m.ParseError` no longer catches an unterminated quote.\n44\t- Not triggered by the current corpus: no `--opt='...\"...'` form in `scripts/*.sh`. It is latent.\n45\t- Reproduction: load `.claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py` with `spec_from_file_location`, then:\n46\t  - `issubclass(m.Incomplete, m.ParseError)` should print False (True before the split);\n47\t  - `m.canon_operand('a\"b')` should raise (it returned before).\n48\t- Minimal fix: in `_bind_class`, return exception classes unchanged (`if issubclass(original, BaseException): return original`), or remap `__bases__` through the clones already bound in the namespace. Add tests for `issubclass(m.Incomplete, m.ParseError)` and for a census fixture containing `sort --output='a\"b' x`.\n49\t\n50\tP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n51\t\n52\t1. Cost-transcripts dataclass `repr()` breaks.\n53\t   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_cost_transcripts_parts/__init__.py:54-56`.\n54\t   - Cause: the binder rebinds every class function whose `__module__` matches the template module. That includes the dataclass `__repr__` wrapper, which `functools.wraps` stamps with the template's `__module__`. Rebound to the entry point's globals, the wrapper's global `_thread` (`dataclasses._recursive_repr`) no longer resolves.\n55\t   - Effect: `repr()` of `UsageRecord`, `ScanCounters`, `PricingResult` or `Priced` raises `NameError`. `transcript_rollup()` returns `pricing_result` and `counters` instances to programmatic callers, so `print(rolled)`, a debugger or a pytest failure message crashes.\n56\t   - No production renderer calls repr on them today.\n57\t   - Fix: do not clone classes that have no user-defined methods, or skip functions that carry `__wrapped__`, or check `__code__.co_filename`.\n58\t\n59\t2. Property getters keep the template's globals.\n60\t   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:29-35`.\n61\t   - Cause: `_bind_class` rebinds plain functions, staticmethods and classmethods, but not `property` objects.\n62\t   - Effect: `Site.blocking` (`report.py:32-34`) reads `BLOCKING_VERDICTS` from the template module, while `main()` reads the entry point's. Patching `m.BLOCKING_VERDICTS` changes one and not the other, which moves a test patch point.\n63\t   - Fix: rebind `fget`, `fset` and `fdel` too.\n64\t\n65\t3. Stale location claims, one of them in machine-readable output.\n66\t   - `middle_out.py:22` and `summarization.py:21` still say the code \"lives here in `scripts/`\" (so does `compaction.py:20`, outside this payload).\n67\t   - `status.py:24` refers to \"probes above\".\n68\t   - The `removal_scope` strings at `status.py:70,98,133` (\"this block\", \"the D2 block in this file\") are emitted by `--probe-status` and `--json`. They now point a reader at a file that no longer holds the code.\n69\t   - Fix: name the module paths.\n70\t\n71\t4. Section headers and doc comments left behind by the split.\n72\t   - `routing.py:307-321` ends with the \"Programmatic API\" header and the `#:` doc comment for `ROOT_ENV`, which now lives in `rollup.py`.\n73\t   - `pricing.py` and `rollup.py` end with headers for code that is in other files.\n74\t   - Cosmetic only.\n75\t\n76\t5. Wrong repo-root fallback in the census engine.\n77\t   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/engine.py:115`.\n78\t   - The fallback `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]` is `.claude`, not the repo root, for the `hooks/_lib` layout.\n79\t   - It is only reached when no `.claude` ancestor holds a `scripts/` directory, which would also leave the `--repo-root` default wrong.\n80\t   - Fix: use `parents[4]`, or fail loudly.\n81\t\n82\tVERDICT: GO-WITH-CONDITIONS No declared condition is contradicted by this payload and no P0 was found, but one undeclared P1 must go into the signed annex: the installer-census entry point splits the ParseError/Incomplete hierarchy, so inputs the old script handled now crash the CI gate instead of becoming blocking sites (the gate still fails closed).\n83\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39b2a95c-7098-4a31-b7e8-36b062b92a5a","uuid":"34a3ef73-fe8b-40f4-9264-1ae2c6012c0a","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:25:29.018Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-08.verdict.txt","content":"PAYLOAD 8/32: review of v1.4.3-rc.1..0e687e3c5285\n\nScope read. The new PLAN-197 split modules are `.claude/hooks/_lib/_context_budget_parts/{middle_out,status,summarization}.py`, `_cost_transcripts_parts/{__init__,cli,pricing,records,rollup,routing,routing_helpers}.py` and `_installer_write_safety/{__init__,commands,engine,model,operands}.py`. I also read the three entry points that load them (`.claude/scripts/context-budget.py:199-203`, `.claude/scripts/ceo-cost-transcripts.py:248-252`, `.claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py:23-32`), the sibling modules they bind together (inventory/compaction, syntax/verdicts_a/verdicts_b/report), the fail-soft loader `ceo-cost.py:483-510`, and the CI gate `validate.yml:411-435`.\n\nLimit of this review: I had read-only file tools and no shell. Every finding below comes from reading the code against CPython 3.9 semantics. Each one comes with a short reproduction the maintainers can run.\n\nDeclared conditions this payload touches:\n- Condition 7: true for these three entry points. They keep their paths, and the logic now lives in `_lib` packages.\n- Condition 5: none of these three packages is in the 13 new kernel lines, and the condition does not claim them.\n- Condition 13: every path in this payload is in scope.\n\nNo declared condition is contradicted, and I found no P0.\n\nAdopter blast radius:\n- No hook, settings template, model, effort or session gate changes in this payload.\n- `context-budget.py` is an advisory CLI behind `/context-budget`.\n- `ceo-cost-transcripts.py` is loaded fail-soft by `ceo-cost.py:495-510`.\n- `check-installer-write-safety.py` is a maintainer CI census (`validate.yml:411-414`, `set -euo pipefail`), so any crash leaves CI red.\n- `install_lib_selective` (`scripts/install.sh:1520-1547`) copies each top-level `_lib` entry, so the three new packages ship on install.\n\nPython 3.9 and stdlib-only: confirmed. No 3.10+ syntax or APIs. Imports are standard library plus `_lib.runtime_paths`, `_lib.agent_frontmatter` and `_lib.injection_patterns`.\n\nName resolution: every name the split modules leave undefined resolves in the entry point's namespace after binding:\n- context-budget: `_env_int` and `_clamp_pct` come from compaction, `_clamp_nonneg_int` from summarization, `estimate_tokens` and `_read_text` from inventory. `CAT_MCP` and `THRESHOLD_SKILL_LINES` are defined in the entry point.\n- cost-transcripts: the entry point defines `_SCRIPT_DIR` and `_rp`, and imports every stdlib name the binder excludes.\n\nNo duplicate top-level definitions across components, so binding order does not change which definition wins.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n\nP1. The installer-census entry point splits the ParseError/Incomplete exception hierarchy.\n- Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:36`.\n- Cause: `_bind_class` rebuilds every package class with `type(original)(original.__name__, original.__bases__, attrs)`, keeping the original bases. In the entry point's namespace, `ParseError` becomes a fresh clone. `Incomplete` (`syntax.py:10`, `class Incomplete(ParseError)`) becomes a clone whose base is the template `syntax.ParseError`, not that clone. So in the entry point, `issubclass(Incomplete, ParseError)` is now False; in the old single-file script it was True.\n- Effect: the rebound `_read_dquote` and `_read_balanced` raise the `Incomplete` clone (`syntax.py:44,50,63,75,89,93`), and every rebound `except ParseError:` stops catching it: `operands.py:35`, `commands.py:131`, `model.py:320,344,632`, `verdicts_b.py:141,440,447`. Only `syntax.py:270`, which names `Incomplete` itself, still catches it.\n- Failure scenario: a scanned `scripts/**/*.sh` line such as `sort --output='a\"b' in.txt`, `curl --output='x\"y' \"$u\"` or `cp --target-directory='d\"x' f`.\n  1. The tokenizer accepts the line.\n  2. `_classify_output_mode` (`commands.py:571`) or the writer branch (`commands.py:478`) rebuilds the canonical value `a\"b` as a new token.\n  3. `_record_command` → `_record_occ` → `canon_operand` → `_read_dquote` raises `Incomplete`, which its `except ParseError` no longer catches.\n  4. The exception escapes `build_file_model` and `run_census` (no broad except anywhere in the package). The census dies with a traceback and exit 1, emitting no site and naming no file.\n  \n  The old script fell back and kept scanning. The same applies to `_expansion_refs` on a sed side holding `${x:-it's}`.\n- Fail direction: still closed, because the CI gate goes red. But the instrument's own contract is broken: an input it cannot handle should become a blocking site, and it now becomes a crash.\n- It also contradicts CHANGELOG.md:112 (\"Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts\"), which names `check-installer-write-safety.py` at :116-117. The entry point's docstring also promises downstream harnesses the parser helpers, and `try: m.lex(t) except m.ParseError` no longer catches an unterminated quote.\n- Not triggered by the current corpus: no `--opt='...\"...'` form in `scripts/*.sh`. It is latent.\n- Reproduction: load `.claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py` with `spec_from_file_location`, then:\n  - `issubclass(m.Incomplete, m.ParseError)` should print False (True before the split);\n  - `m.canon_operand('a\"b')` should raise (it returned before).\n- Minimal fix: in `_bind_class`, return exception classes unchanged (`if issubclass(original, BaseException): return original`), or remap `__bases__` through the clones already bound in the namespace. Add tests for `issubclass(m.Incomplete, m.ParseError)` and for a census fixture containing `sort --output='a\"b' x`.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n\n1. Cost-transcripts dataclass `repr()` breaks.\n   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_cost_transcripts_parts/__init__.py:54-56`.\n   - Cause: the binder rebinds every class function whose `__module__` matches the template module. That includes the dataclass `__repr__` wrapper, which `functools.wraps` stamps with the template's `__module__`. Rebound to the entry point's globals, the wrapper's global `_thread` (`dataclasses._recursive_repr`) no longer resolves.\n   - Effect: `repr()` of `UsageRecord`, `ScanCounters`, `PricingResult` or `Priced` raises `NameError`. `transcript_rollup()` returns `pricing_result` and `counters` instances to programmatic callers, so `print(rolled)`, a debugger or a pytest failure message crashes.\n   - No production renderer calls repr on them today.\n   - Fix: do not clone classes that have no user-defined methods, or skip functions that carry `__wrapped__`, or check `__code__.co_filename`.\n\n2. Property getters keep the template's globals.\n   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:29-35`.\n   - Cause: `_bind_class` rebinds plain functions, staticmethods and classmethods, but not `property` objects.\n   - Effect: `Site.blocking` (`report.py:32-34`) reads `BLOCKING_VERDICTS` from the template module, while `main()` reads the entry point's. Patching `m.BLOCKING_VERDICTS` changes one and not the other, which moves a test patch point.\n   - Fix: rebind `fget`, `fset` and `fdel` too.\n\n3. Stale location claims, one of them in machine-readable output.\n   - `middle_out.py:22` and `summarization.py:21` still say the code \"lives here in `scripts/`\" (so does `compaction.py:20`, outside this payload).\n   - `status.py:24` refers to \"probes above\".\n   - The `removal_scope` strings at `status.py:70,98,133` (\"this block\", \"the D2 block in this file\") are emitted by `--probe-status` and `--json`. They now point a reader at a file that no longer holds the code.\n   - Fix: name the module paths.\n\n4. Section headers and doc comments left behind by the split.\n   - `routing.py:307-321` ends with the \"Programmatic API\" header and the `#:` doc comment for `ROOT_ENV`, which now lives in `rollup.py`.\n   - `pricing.py` and `rollup.py` end with headers for code that is in other files.\n   - Cosmetic only.\n\n5. Wrong repo-root fallback in the census engine.\n   - Where: `.claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/engine.py:115`.\n   - The fallback `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]` is `.claude`, not the repo root, for the `hooks/_lib` layout.\n   - It is only reached when no `.claude` ancestor holds a `scripts/` directory, which would also leave the `--repo-root` default wrong.\n   - Fix: use `parents[4]`, or fail loudly.\n\nVERDICT: GO-WITH-CONDITIONS No declared condition is contradicted by this payload and no P0 was found, but one undeclared P1 must go into the signed annex: the installer-census entry point splits the ParseError/Incomplete hierarchy, so inputs the old script handled now crash the CI gate instead of becoming blocking sites (the gate still fails closed).\n","numLines":83,"startLine":1,"totalLines":83}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DXWQakvvbJZXfFrxEoQUA5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
